ny lines total, and vertical lines are non-sunny. So we can have exactly n-2 vertical lines, and no other non-sunny lines. That means all non-sunny lines must be vertical. So any non-vertical non-sunny line (horizontal or slope -1) would reduce the number of vertical lines, making it impossible to cover all columns. So indeed, all non-sunny lines must be vertical. So we must have n-2 vertical lines. Since we have n columns, we need to leave exactly 2 columns uncovered by vertical lines, which must be covered by the 2 sunny lines. So the only possible configuration for k=2 is: n-2 vertical lines covering all columns except two, and 2 sunny lines covering the remaining columns. Let's see if that can be done.\n\nWe have n columns: x=1,...,x=n. Choose two columns to be covered by sunny lines. Let's denote them as columns i and j. The vertical lines cover all other columns. For each column that is not vertical, we need to cover all its points using sunny lines. Since a sunny line can intersect a column at most one point, each column not covered by a vertical line must have each of its points covered by distinct sunny lines. But we have only 2 sunny lines, so each of those columns can have at most 2 points total (since each sunny line can intersect that column at most once). So the columns we leave uncovered must have at most 2 points each. Which columns have at most 2 points? Column n (size 1) and column n-1 (size 2). Column n-2 has size 3, which is too many. So the only possible columns to leave uncovered are n and n-1. That would be two columns, each with ≤2 points. So we could have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2 (that's n-2 vertical lines). Then we have two sunny lines to cover column n-1 (two points) and column n (one point). But we have only two sunny lines, so we need to cover three points (two in column n-1 and one in column n) with two lines. Since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, we could assign one sunny line to cover (n,1) and mayb