hat intersect column c is at most k (since each sunny line can intersect column c at most once). So if h_c > k, we need at least one vertical line to cover the remaining points. Actually we need at least h_c - k vertical lines? Let's think: each vertical line can cover all points in that column, but we only need one vertical line to cover all points not covered by sunny lines. However, if we have multiple vertical lines, they would be redundant. So for each column c, if h_c > k, we need at least one vertical line (non-sunny) covering that column. So any column with more than k points must have a vertical line. Since h_c decreases as c increases, the columns with many points are the leftmost columns. For k≥4, columns with h_c > k are those with c ≤ n+1 - (k+1) = n - k. Actually h_c = n+1-c > k => c < n+1 - k => c ≤ n - k. So columns 1,...,n-k must have vertical lines. That's n-k columns requiring vertical lines. Since we have n-k non-sunny lines total, we can allocate exactly one vertical line per each of those columns, and the remaining non-sunny lines (if any) could be horizontal or slope -1. So it's possible to have exactly n-k vertical lines covering columns 1,...,n-k, and the remaining columns n-k+1,...,n are covered by sunny lines (or possibly also by horizontal lines). But we have k sunny lines, which can each intersect each column at most once. So we need to cover the points in columns n-k+1,...,n using the k sunny lines. Let's examine the sizes of those columns: column n-k+1 has h = n+1-(n-k+1) = k points? Let's compute: h_{n-k+1} = n+1 - (n-k+1) = n+1 - n + k - 1 = k. So column n-k+1 has exactly k points. Column n-k+2 has k-1 points, etc., down to column n has 1 point. So the columns from n-k+1 to n have sizes k, k-1, ..., 1. So total points in those columns = sum_{i=1}^{k} i = k(k+1)/2. We have k sunny lines, each can intersect each column at most once, so each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So across all columns n-k+1,...,n, each sunny