r slope -1 lines covering diagonals.\n\nWe need to see if any configuration exists. The solution's argument for impossibility of k=2 uses reasoning about columns: column n-2 (i.e., column 2 for n=4) has 3 points (since h_{n-2}=3). Actually for n=4, columns: x=1 has h_1 = n+1-1 = 4 points; x=2 has h_2 = 3 points; x=3 has h_3 = 2 points; x=4 has h_4 = 1 point. So column x=2 has 3 points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). The solution says if k≤2 then column n-2 (x=n-2) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; thus one of the non-sunny lines must be vertical and must be line x=n-2. Let's examine: If we have at most 2 sunny lines, can we cover column x=2 (3 points) using only sunny lines? Each sunny line can intersect column x=2 at at most one point (since not vertical). So with 2 sunny lines, we can cover at most 2 points in column x=2. So we need at least one non-sunny line to cover the remaining point(s). The non-sunny line could be vertical x=2 (covers all 3 points), or could be horizontal covering one of them, or slope -1 covering maybe one or two? Let's see: Horizontal line y=1 covers (2,1) but not (2,2),(2,3). Slope -1 line x+y=3 passes through (2,1) and (1,2) but not (2,2) or (2,3). So to cover all three points, we need either vertical line x=2 (covers all three) or a combination of non-sunny lines: e.g., horizontal y=1 covers (2,1), slope -1 line x+y=4 covers (2,2) and (1,3) maybe, but (2,3) remains uncovered. So we need at least one vertical line x=2. So indeed one non-sunny line must be vertical x=2.\n\nNow the solution continues: \"Now look at column x=n-1 (two points). If we do not use a vertical line x=n-1, both its points must be covered by sunny lines, i.e., each sunny line must contain exactly one of them. The remaining column x=n contains a single point (n,1). A line joining (n,1) with either of the two points of column n-1 is either horizontal (slope 0) or has slope -1; both are non-sunny. Hence (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point of column