e points in the three rightmost columns: column n (size 1) point (n,1); column n-1 (size 2) points (n-1,1),(n-1,2); column n-2 (size 3) points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). So the three sunny lines cover all points in the rightmost three columns.\n\nThen they add vertical lines for columns x=1,2,...,n-3 (non-sunny). Those vertical lines cover all remaining points. Since there are n-3 vertical lines plus 3 sunny lines = n lines total. So this construction works for any n≥3. Indeed, for n=3, the vertical lines are x=1,...,0? Actually n-3 = 0, so no vertical lines, just the three sunny lines. Let's check for n=3: The rightmost three columns are columns 1,2,3? Actually n=3, columns are x=1,2,3. The three rightmost columns are all columns. The construction uses L1, L2, L3 as defined. Let's check if they cover all points for n=3.\n\nPoints T_3: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's 6 points. The three sunny lines:\n\n- L1: y-1 = -1/2 (x-3). At x=3, y=1. At x=1, x-3 = -2 => y-1 = -1/2 * (-2) = 1 => y=2. So L1 passes through (3,1) and (1,2). Good.\n\n- L2: y-1 = -2 (x-2). At x=2, y=1. At x=1, x-2 = -1 => y-1 = -2 * (-1) = 2 => y=3. So L2 passes through (2,1) and (1,3). Good.\n\n- L3: y-1 = (x-1). At x=1, y=1. At x=2, x-1=1 => y-1 = 1 => y=2. So L3 passes through (1,1) and (2,2). So the three lines cover all six points. So works for n=3.\n\nThus k=3 construction works.\n\nNow k=1 construction: vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1 plus a sunny line L: y-1 = 1/(1-n) (x-n). Let's check that line passes through (n,1). Indeed at x=n, y=1. At x=n-1, x-n = -1 => y-1 = 1/(1-n) * (-1) = -1/(1-n) = 1/(n-1). Since n≥3, n-1≥2, so y = 1 + 1/(n-1) which is not integer unless n-1=1 (n=2) which is not allowed. So the line only passes through (n,1) among T_n points. So all other points are covered by vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1. Indeed, for any point (a,b) with a ≤ n-1, it's on vertical line x=a. So all points covered. So k=1 works.\n\nk=0 construction: all vertical lines x=1,...,x=n. Works.\n\nTh