sed n-3 vertical lines, leaving 3 lines. Among those 3 lines, we need exactly 2 sunny lines, so we have 1 non-sunny line left. So we need to cover the 6 points with 2 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line. Is that possible? Let's see.\n\nWe need to cover column n-2 (3 points) with at most 2 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line. The non-sunny line could be vertical x=n-2 covering all 3 points. Then we have 2 sunny lines left to cover columns n-1 and n. As before, we cannot cover (n,1) and (n-1,1) with a sunny line, and we cannot cover both points in column n-1 with a sunny line. So we need to use non-sunny lines for column n-1 and n. But we have no non-sunny lines left. So this fails.\n\nThus we need to allocate non-sunny lines differently: maybe use a diagonal line to cover some points in column n-2 and n-1 simultaneously, freeing up a vertical line for column n-2? Let's explore.\n\nWe have 2 sunny lines. Let's try to use a diagonal line (non-sunny) x+y = n (which passes through (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) and (n-3,3) ...). This diagonal covers points (i, n-i) for i from 1 to n-1. But note that points (i, n-i) are in the set only if i + (n-i) = n ≤ n+1, which is true. So diagonal x+y=n includes points (1,n-1),(2,n-2),...,(n-1,1). Among these, many are in the set. This diagonal is non-sunny (slope -1). It covers one point in each column from 1 to n-1. So it can reduce the need for vertical lines for those columns.\n\nSimilarly, we could use diagonal x+y=n+1 (slope -1) covering points (1,n),(2,n-1),...,(n,1). But (1,n) is not in the set because b must be ≤ n+1-a, so for a=1, b ≤ n, so (1,n) is allowed (since a+b = n+1). So diagonal x+y=n+1 includes points (1,n),(2,n-1),...,(n,1). So that diagonal covers the rightmost column point (n,1) and also many other points.\n\nThus we could use diagonal lines as non-sunny lines to cover many points, leaving fewer points for sunny lines.\n\nBut we have only n-2 non-sunny lines. We could use two diagonal lines (x+y=n and x+y=n+1) as non-sunny line