tion says yes, but we need to verify.\n\nWe need to find a general construction for k=3. Let's try to design one.\n\nWe have n lines total, with exactly 3 sunny lines. So we have n-3 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal. We need to cover all points.\n\nOne approach: Use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 vertical lines). That covers all points in those columns. Then we have columns n-2, n-1, n left. These columns have at most 3 points each. We have 3 sunny lines left to cover those points. Since each column has at most 3 points, we can assign each point in those columns to a distinct sunny line, ensuring each sunny line covers at most one point per column. However, we need to ensure that each sunny line can cover points across multiple columns (maybe one per column). But we have 3 sunny lines, and we have at most 3 columns left, each with up to 3 points. So we could assign each sunny line to cover one point in each of the three columns, covering all points. But we need to ensure that the three points assigned to a given sunny line are collinear (i.e., lie on a single line). Since we can choose any line, we can try to pick three points, one from each column, that are collinear and not parallel to axes or slope -1. Is that always possible? Let's examine.\n\nColumns:\n\n- Column n-2: points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3).\n- Column n-1: points (n-1,1), (n-1,2).\n- Column n: point (n,1).\n\nWe need to assign each of the three sunny lines to cover some points. Since we have three sunny lines, we could assign each sunny line to cover exactly one point in each column, i.e., each sunny line passes through three points: one from column n-2, one from column n-1, one from column n. That would cover all points in those columns (since each column has at most 3 points). However, column n-1 has only 2 points, so one of the sunny lines would not have a point from column n-1? Actually we have 3 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So we have at most 3 points covered in column n-2 (which has exactly 3 points), at most 3 points covered in column n-1 (which has 2 points), and at most 3 points covered in column n (which has 1 point). So we can cover all points in those columns if we assign each point to a distinct sunny line. For column n-2, we need to assign each of its 3 points to a different sunny line. For column n-1, we need to assign its 2 points to two of the sunny lines (the third sunny line will not have a point in column n-1). For column n, we assign its single point to one of the sunny lines (maybe the same as one of the other columns). So we need to find three lines (sunny) such that each line passes through a point in column n-2, and possibly also passes through a point in column n-1 and/or column n. But we need each line to be straight, so each line can pass through at most two points (unless they are collinear). Actually a line can pass through any number of points, but we need to assign points to lines such that each line passes through the assigned points. So we need to find three lines that collectively cover all points in columns n-2, n-1, n. Since there are 6 points total (3+2+1=6). We have 3 lines, each can cover multiple points. Ideally each line could cover 2 points, covering all 6 points. But we need to ensure each line is sunny (i.e., not horizontal, vertical, or slope -1). So we need to find a pairing of points into 3 lines, each line being sunny.\n\nOne simple construction: Use three sunny lines each covering two points: pair (n-2,1) with (n-1,2) maybe? Let's see slope: (2-1)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 1/1 = 1, which is sunny (slope 1). But slope 1 is not prohibited (only 0, ∞, -1 are prohibited). So line L1: through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) has slope 1, sunny. It also passes through (n,3) which is not in our set (since column n has only point (n,1)). So L1 covers (n-2,1) and (n-1,2). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover remaining points: (n-2,2), (n-2,3), (n-1,1), (n,1). We have two sunny lines left.\n\nWe could pair (n-2,2) with (n,1): slope = (1-2)/(n-(n-2)) = (-1)/2 = -1/2, which is sunny. So L2: line through (n-2,2) and (n,1). That covers those two points.\n\nNow remaining points: (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). Pair them: slope = (1-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = (-2)/1 = -2, which is sunny. So L3: line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). That covers those two points.\n\nThus we have three sunny lines covering all points in columns n-2, n-1, n. So the construction works for any n≥3. Indeed, we used vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 lines), and three sunny lines as above. So total lines = (n-3) + 3 = n. Exactly 3 sunny lines. So k=3 is possible.\n\nThus answer {0,1,3} seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to check if k≥4 is impossible. The solution says \"sunny lines cover at most one point per column.\" So with k≥4, we have at least 4 sunny lines. Each column can have at most 4 points covered by sunny lines. But columns with many points (like column 1) have n points. So we need non-sunny lines to cover the rest. We have n-k non-sunny lines. For k≥4, n-k ≤ n-4. So we have at most n-4 non-sunny lines. We need to cover the remaining points in each column after using sunny lines. Let's see if we can prove impossibility.\n\nWe need to show that for any configuration with k≥4 sunny lines, we cannot cover all points with total n lines. Equivalent to: n-k non-sunny lines cannot cover all points not covered by sunny lines. Since each non-sunny line can be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal. Each non-sunny line can cover many points, but we need to see if n-k non-sunny lines can cover all points in columns where sunny lines cover at most k points per column.\n\nWe need to find a lower bound on the number of non-sunny lines needed to cover the points, given k sunny lines. If that lower bound > n-k, then impossible.\n\nOne approach: Count points per column. Column a has n+1-a points. Sunny lines can cover at most k points in column a (since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column). So the remaining points in column a that must be covered by non-sunny lines is at least max(0, (n+1-a) - k). Summing over all columns a = 1,...,n, we get total points that must be covered by non-sunny lines: sum_{a=1}^{n} max(0, (n+1-a) - k). Since (n+1-a) decreases from n to 1. For a ≤ n+1-k, (n+1-a) - k ≥ 0. For a > n+1-k, (n+1-a) - k < 0, so max = 0. So columns a = 1,...,n+1-k have at least (n+1-a)-k points to be covered by non-sunny lines. So total points to be covered by non-sunny lines = sum_{a=1}^{n+1-k} ((n+1-a)-k) = sum_{t=1}^{n+1-k} (t - k) where t = n+1-a. Actually let's rewrite: For a from 1 to n+1-k, n+1-a runs from n to k+? Let's compute: When a=1, n+1-a = n. When a=n+1-k, n+1-a = k. So the values are n, n-1, ..., k. So sum_{a=1}^{n+1-k} ((n+1-a)-k) = sum_{j=k}^{n} (j - k) where j = n+1-a. So sum_{j=k}^{n} (j - k) = sum_{i=0}^{n-k} i = (n-k)(n-k+1)/2. So total points that must be covered by non-sunny lines is (n-k)(n-k+1)/2.\n\nNow each non-sunny line can cover at most how many points? A vertical line can cover all points in a column, which could be up to n points. A horizontal line can cover all points in a row, up to n points. A diagonal line can cover up to n points (the longest diagonal from (1,n) to (n,1) has n points). So each non-sunny line can cover at most n points. But we have n-k non-sunny lines. So total capacity of non-sunny lines is at most (n-k)*n. But we need to cover (n-k)(n-k+1)/2 points. Since (n-k)*n is larger than that for any n≥? Actually (n-k)*n >= (n-k)(n-k+1)/2 for all n≥? Let's check: Multiply both sides by 2: 2n(n-k) >= (n-k)(n-k+1). Cancel (n-k) (assuming n>k). Then 2n >= n-k+1 => 2n >= n - k + 1 => n >= -k + 1 => n + k >= 1, which is always true. So capacity bound is not restrictive.\n\nBut we need to consider that non-sunny lines cannot be arbitrarily placed; they must be distinct lines, and each line can cover points in multiple columns/rows/diagonals. However, we need to consider that each non-sunny line can cover at most one point per column if it's not vertical? Actually vertical lines can cover many points in a column, but they cover only one column each. Horizontal lines cover many columns but only one point per column? Wait, horizontal line y = constant covers points across many columns, but each column has at most one point on that horizontal line (since each column has at most one point with that y coordinate). So horizontal lines also cover at most one point per column. Similarly, diagonal lines x+y = constant also cover at most one point per column. So any non-sunny line (vertical, horizontal, diagonal) covers at most one point per column. However, vertical lines cover all points in a column, which is many points per column, but they are all in the same column. So vertical lines can cover many points in a single column, but they cannot cover points in other columns. Horizontal lines can cover many points across many columns, but only one per column. Diagonal lines also cover at most one per column.\n\nThus, the total number of points covered by non-sunny lines can be counted by column contributions: each column can be covered by at most one vertical line (if we choose a vertical line for that column) plus possibly horizontal and diagonal lines each covering at most one point in that column. So each column can have at most (1 vertical + 1 horizontal + 1 diagonal) = 3 points covered by non-sunny lines? Actually a column can be intersected by a vertical line (covering all points in that column), plus a horizontal line (covering one point in that column), plus a diagonal line (covering one point in that column). However, if we have a vertical line covering all points in that column, we don't need horizontal or diagonal lines for that column. But we might have horizontal and diagonal lines that also intersect that column at points that are already covered by the vertical line, which would be redundant. So we need to consider the maximum number of distinct points in a column that can be covered by non-sunny lines without redundancy. If we have a vertical line for column a, it covers all points in that column. So we don't need any other lines for that column. So each column can be covered by at most one vertical line (if we choose to use a vertical line for that column). If we don't use a vertical line for a column, we can cover at most two points in that column using a horizontal line and a diagonal line (since each can intersect the column at one point). So each column can have at most 2 points covered by non-sunny lines if we don't use a vertical line. So the maximum number of points covered by non-sunny lines across all columns is: sum_{columns with vertical line} (n+1-a) + sum_{columns without vertical line} (max 2). But we have limited number of vertical lines (non-sunny lines). Actually we have n-k non-sunny lines total, which can be allocated among vertical, horizontal, diagonal. So we need to allocate them to maximize coverage.\n\nBut perhaps a more direct approach: The solution's argument for k≥4 is that each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so with k≥4, we have at most 4 points per column covered by sunny lines. The remaining points per column must be covered by non-sunny lines. Since we have n-k ≤ n-4 non-sunny lines, we need to cover many points. But maybe we can show that we need at least n-3 non-sunny lines to cover all points, so k cannot be ≥4. Let's try to prove minimal number of non-sunny lines needed.\n\nObservation: Column 1 has n points. Sunny lines can cover at most k points in column 1. So we need to cover the remaining n - k points in column 1 using non-sunny lines. Since each non-sunny line can cover at most one point in column 1 unless it's vertical (which would cover all points in column 1). So we have two options: either we use a vertical line for column 1, covering all n points, or we use other non-sunny lines (horizontal and diagonal) each covering at most one point in column 1. So to cover n - k points in column 1, we need either a vertical line (covers all) or at least n - k non-sunny lines (horizontal/diagonal) intersecting column 1 at distinct points. Since we have only n - k non-sunny lines total, we need to allocate them wisely.\n\nIf we use a vertical line for column 1, that uses one non-sunny line. Then we have n - k - 1 non-sunny lines left to cover other columns.\n\nIf we don't use a vertical line for column 1, we need at least n - k non-sunny lines intersecting column 1 at distinct points. Since we have only n - k non-sunny lines total, that would use all of them just for column 1, leaving none for other columns. So we must use a vertical line for column 1. So we need at least one vertical line.\n\nSimilarly, consider column 2, which has n-1 points. Sunny lines can cover at most k points in column 2. So we need to cover n-1 - k points in column 2 using non-sunny lines. If we have a vertical line for column 2, that covers all points in column 2. That would use another non-sunny line. If we don't have a vertical line for column 2, we need at least n-1 - k non-sunny lines intersecting column 2 at distinct points. Since we have limited non-sunny lines, we might need vertical lines for many columns.\n\nThus, to minimize the number of non-sunny lines, we should use vertical lines for columns where the number of points left after sunny lines is large. So we need to decide which columns get vertical lines.\n\nGiven k sunny lines, we can assign them to cover at most k points per column. So for column a, we need to cover n+1-a - k points with non-sunny lines. If we assign a vertical line to column a, we cover all points in that column, so we don't need any other non-sunny lines for that column. So we can assign vertical lines to columns where n+1-a - k > 0 (i.e., columns with more points than k). For columns where n+1-a ≤ k, we don't need vertical lines because sunny lines can cover all points in that column (since there are at most k points). However, we need to ensure that the sunny lines can be arranged to cover those points appropriately.\n\nThus, the minimal number of vertical lines needed is the number of columns a such that n+1-a > k, i.e., a < n+1 - k. So columns a = 1,...,n - k (since a integer). Actually n+1-a > k => a < n+1 - k => a ≤ n - k (since a integer). So we need vertical lines for columns a = 1,...,n - k. That's n - k columns. So we need at least n - k vertical lines. But we have only n - k non-sunny lines total. So we must use all non-sunny lines as vertical lines for columns 1,...,n - k. That uses exactly n - k non-sunny lines. So we cannot have any horizontal or diagonal non-sunny lines. So the only possible configuration for given k is: use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n - k, and use k sunny lines to cover the remaining points in columns n - k + 1,...,n.\n\nNow we need to check if this is feasible: For columns a > n - k, we have at most k points per column (since a ≥ n - k + 1 => n+1-a ≤ k). So each column has at most k points. We have k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So we need to assign each point in those columns to a distinct sunny line. Since each column has at most k points, we can assign each point to a distinct sunny line, but we need to ensure that each sunny line can pass through points from multiple columns (i.e., each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover points from many columns). So we need to find k lines such that each column's points are assigned to distinct lines, and each line passes through exactly one point from each column (maybe some columns have fewer points, so some lines may not have a point in that column). This is essentially a problem of constructing a system of k lines that intersect each of the columns n - k + 1,...,n at distinct points, covering all points in those columns.\n\nWe need to check if such a construction exists for k=2,3,4,... For k=2, we need to cover columns n-1 and n (since n - k = n - 2). Column n-1 has 2 points, column n has 1 point. So total points = 3. We have 2 sunny lines. Each line can cover at most one point per column. So we need to assign points to lines: we have 2 lines, each can cover at most one point in column n-1, so we can cover at most 2 points in column n-1, which is fine (there are exactly 2). In column n, we have 1 point, which can be covered by either line. So we need to assign points to lines such that each line passes through points in both columns (maybe). But we need to ensure that the points assigned to each line are collinear. So we need to find two lines such that they cover the three points: (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). Is that possible? Let's try to assign: line L1 passes through (n-1,1) and (n,1) => horizontal line y=1, which is non-sunny (slope 0). So not allowed. So we cannot assign both (n-1,1) and (n,1) to a sunny line. So we need to assign (n-1,1) to one sunny line, (n-1,2) to another sunny line, and (n,1) to one of them (maybe the same as (n-1,1) or (n-1,2)). But we cannot assign (n-1,1) and (n,1) to the same sunny line because that line would be horizontal. So we must assign (n,1) to a different sunny line than (n-1,1). So we need three distinct sunny lines to cover three points, but we only have two. So impossible. So k=2 is impossible. This matches the solution.\n\nNow for k=3, we need to cover columns n-2, n-1, n. Column n-2 has 3 points, column n-1 has 2 points, column n has 1 point. Total points = 6. We have 3 sunny lines. Each line can cover at most one point per column. So each column's points must be assigned to distinct lines. For column n-2 (3 points), we need each of the 3 lines to cover one of those points. For column n-1 (2 points), we need two of the lines to cover those points (the third line may not have a point in that column). For column n (1 point), one of the lines covers it. So we need to find 3 lines such that they pass through the assigned points. The solution gave a construction: pair points as we did earlier: (n-2,1)-(n-1,2), (n-2,2)-(n,1), (n-2,3)-(n-1,1). Those are three lines, each sunny. So k=3 works.\n\nNow for k=4, we need to cover columns n-3, n-2, n-1, n. Column n-3 has 4 points, column n-2 has 3 points, column n-1 has 2 points, column n has 1 point. Total points = 4+3+2+1 = 10. We have 4 sunny lines. Each line can cover at most one point per column. So we need to assign each column's points to distinct lines. For column n-3 (4 points), we need each of the 4 lines to cover one point. For column n-2 (3 points), we need 3 of the lines to cover those points. For column n-1 (2 points), we need 2 lines. For column n (1 point), we need 1 line. So we need to find 4 lines such that they pass through these points accordingly. Is it possible? Let's try to see if we can assign points to lines such that each line passes through one point in each column (maybe some columns missing). This is like constructing a set of 4 lines that intersect the columns at distinct points covering all points. This is reminiscent of a combinatorial design: we need to find a set of 4 lines that intersect each of the columns n-3,...,n at distinct points covering all points. This is like a Latin rectangle? Let's try to see if it's possible.\n\nWe can think of columns as x-values: x = n-3, n-2, n-1, n. Points in column x = a are (a,b) where b = 1,...,n+1-a. So for column n-3, b = 1,...,4. So points: (n-3,1), (n-3,2), (n-3,3), (n-3,4). For column n-2: (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). For column n-1: (n-1,1),(n-1,2). For column n: (n,1).\n\nWe need 4 lines L1,...,L4. Each line can intersect each column at at most one point. So each line can be assigned a point from each column (maybe none). The assignment must be such that each point is assigned to exactly one line. So we need to partition the set of points into 4 subsets, each subset being a set of points that lie on a common line (sunny). So we need to partition the 10 points into 4 lines. Since each line can have at most 4 points (one per column), the total capacity is at most 4*4 = 16, which is enough. But we need to find a partition.\n\nWe can try to find a construction. Let's try to assign points to lines as follows: For each line, we can try to pick a point from each column such that they are collinear. For example, we could try to pick points (n-3,1), (n-2,2), (n-1,3?), but (n-1,3) does not exist because column n-1 has only b up to 2. So we cannot have a point in column n-1 with b=3. So we need to be careful.\n\nWe can try to find lines with slope 1 (45 degrees). For slope 1, points (a,b) satisfy b - a = constant. Let's compute b - a for each point:\n\n- Column n-3: points (n-3,1) => b-a = 1-(n-3) = 4-n; (n-3,2) => 5-n; (n-3,3) => 6-n; (n-3,4) => 7-n.\n- Column n-2: (n-2,1) => 3-n; (n-2,2) => 4-n; (n-2,3) => 5-n.\n- Column n-1: (n-1,1) => 2-n; (n-1,2) => 3-n.\n- Column n: (n,1) => 1-n.\n\nSo we have values of b-a ranging from 1-n up to 7-n. For slope 1 lines, points with same b-a are collinear. So we can group points by b-a constant. Let's list them:\n\n- b-a = 7-n: point (n-3,4) only.\n- b-a = 6-n: point (n-3,3) only.\n- b-a = 5-n: points (n-3,2) and (n-2,3). So line with slope 1 through these two points: (n-3,2) and (n-2,3). That line also passes through (n-1,4) which doesn't exist, (n,5) which doesn't exist. So it's a line covering exactly those two points.\n- b-a = 4-n: points (n-3,1) and (n-2,2). Also (n-1,3) doesn't exist. So line with slope 1 covering (n-3,1) and (n-2,2).\n- b-a = 3-n: points (n-2,1) and (n-1,2). Also (n,3) doesn't exist. So line with slope 1 covering (n-2,1) and (n-1,2).\n- b-a = 2-n: point (n-1,1) only.\n- b-a = 1-n: point (n,1) only.\n\nThus we have lines with slope 1 covering pairs: (n-3,2)-(n-2,3), (n-3,1)-(n-2,2), (n-2,1)-(n-1,2). That's three lines. We also have singletons (n-3,4), (n-3,3), (n-1,1), (n,1). That's four more points. So we have total 7 lines if we use slope 1 lines. But we need only 4 lines. So we need to combine some points into lines with other slopes.\n\nAlternatively, we could use lines with slope -2, -1/2, etc. The solution for k=3 used slopes 1, -1/2, -2. For k=4, we might need more lines.\n\nBut perhaps it's impossible to cover all points with 4 sunny lines. The solution claims impossible for k≥4. Let's try to prove impossibility.\n\nWe need to show that for k≥4, we cannot assign points in columns n-k+1,...,n to k lines such that each line is sunny and each column's points are assigned to distinct lines. This is essentially a combinatorial design problem: we have a set of points arranged in a triangular shape, and we need to partition them into k lines, each line intersecting each column at most once. This is reminiscent of a problem about covering a triangular grid with k lines of distinct slopes.\n\nWe can try to prove that for k≥4, the number of points in the rightmost k columns is (k(k+1)/2). We need to partition these points into k lines. Each line can intersect each column at most once, so each line can contain at most k points (one per column). So each line can contain at most k points. So total capacity of k lines is at most k*k = k^2 points. But we have k(k+1)/2 points. For k≥4, k(k+1)/2 > k^2? Let's check: k(k+1)/2 > k^2 => (k+1)/2 > k => k+1 > 2k => 1 > k, which is false for k≥1. So k(k+1)/2 ≤ k^2 for k≥1? Let's compute: k(k+1)/2 = (k^2 + k)/2. Compare to k^2: (k^2 + k)/2 ≤ k^2 => multiply by 2: k^2 + k ≤ 2k^2 => k ≤ k^2 => k^2 - k ≥ 0 => k(k-1) ≥ 0 => true for k≥1. So capacity is enough: k lines can cover up to k^2 points, and we have at most k(k+1)/2 points, which is ≤ k^2 for k≥1. So capacity is not a problem.\n\nBut we also need to consider that each line must be sunny (i.e., slope not 0,∞,-1). So we cannot use horizontal, vertical, or slope -1 lines. So we need to find k lines with slopes not equal to 0,∞,-1 that cover all points in the rightmost k columns. For k=4, we need to find 4 lines that cover the 10 points in columns n-3,...,n. Is that possible? Let's try to find a construction.\n\nWe can try to assign points to lines as follows: Use lines with slopes 1, -1/2, -2, maybe something else. Let's try to see if we can cover all points with 4 lines.\n\nWe have points:\n\nColumn n-3: (n-3,1), (n-3,2), (n-3,3), (n-3,4)\nColumn n-2: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3)\nColumn n-1: (n-1,1), (n-1,2)\nColumn n: (n,1)\n\nWe need to assign each point to one of 4 lines.\n\nOne approach: Use vertical lines for some columns? But vertical lines are non-sunny, not allowed for sunny lines. So we cannot use vertical lines for these columns because we have used all non-sunny lines for columns 1,...,n-4 (vertical lines). So the remaining columns must be covered by sunny lines only. So we cannot use vertical lines for columns n-3,...,n. So we need to use sunny lines.\n\nThus we need to find 4 sunny lines that cover all points in these columns.\n\nWe can try to find a line that passes through (n-3,1) and (n-2,2) (slope 1). That line also passes through (n-1,3) which doesn't exist, (n,4) which doesn't exist. So that line covers exactly those two points.\n\nSimilarly, line through (n-3,2) and (n-2,3) (slope 1) covers those two points.\n\nLine through (n-3,3) and (n-2,4) doesn't exist because (n-2,4) not in set. So (n-3,3) cannot be paired with a point in column n-2 with slope 1. Could pair with (n-1,4) which doesn't exist. So (n-3,3) must be paired with a point in column n-1 or n. Let's try line through (n-3,3) and (n-1,2). Slope = (2-3)/(n-1-(n-3)) = (-1)/2 = -1/2, which is sunny. That line also passes through (n,? ) maybe (n,1.5) not integer. So line L3: (n-3,3)-(n-1,2). That covers those two points.\n\nNow we have left points: (n-3,4), (n-2,1), (n-2,2?), (n-2,3?), (n-1,1), (n,1). Actually we have used (n-3,1),(n-2,2) in L1; (n-3,2),(n-2,3) in L2; (n-3,3),(n-1,2) in L3. So remaining points: (n-3,4), (n-2,1), (n-2,? Actually we used (n-2,2) and (n-2,3). So left (n-2,1). Also (n-1,1) and (n,1). So we have 4 points left: (n-3,4), (n-2,1), (n-1,1), (n,1). We need to cover them with one more sunny line (L4). Is there a sunny line passing through all four? Unlikely. Let's see if we can find a line passing through (n-3,4) and (n,1): slope = (1-4)/(n-(n-3)) = (-3)/3 = -1. That's slope -1, which is not sunny (it's prohibited). So cannot use that line. How about line through (n-3,4) and (n-2,1): slope = (1-4)/(n-2-(n-3)) = (-3)/1 = -3, which is sunny. That line passes through (n-1,? ) maybe (n-1,? ) = y = -3(x - (n-3)) + 4 = -3x + 3(n-3) + 4 = -3x + 3n - 9 + 4 = -3x + 3n -5. For x = n-1, y = -3(n-1) + 3n -5 = -3n + 3 + 3n -5 = -2. So (n-1,-2) not in set. For x = n, y = -3n + 3n -5 = -5, not in set. So line L4 passes through (n-3,4) and (n-2,1) only. That leaves (n-1,1) and (n,1) uncovered. So we need more lines.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe could try different pairings. Let's try to find a line that covers (n-3,4) and (n,1) but slope -1 is prohibited. So we cannot pair those two points on a sunny line. So (n-3,4) cannot be paired with (n,1). It could be paired with (n-1,1) maybe? Slope = (1-4)/(n-1-(n-3)) = (-3)/2 = -3/2, which is sunny. That line passes through (n-2,? ) maybe (n-2,? ) = y = -3/2 (x - (n-3)) + 4 = -3/2 (x - n + 3) + 4 = -3/2 x + (3/2)(n-3) + 4. For x = n-2, y = -3/2 (n-2) + (3/2)(n-3) + 4 = -3/2 n + 3 + (3/2 n - 9/2) + 4 = combine: (-3/2 n + 3/2 n) = -n/2? Actually -3/2 n + (3/2 n) = 0. So we have 3 - 9/2 + 4 = 3 + 4 - 4.5 = 2.5? Let's compute precisely: 3 - 9/2 = 3 - 4.5 = -1.5. Then +4 = 2.5. So y = 2.5, not integer. So line does not pass through any integer point in column n-2. So L4 would cover only (n-3,4) and (n-1,1). Then we still have (n-2,1), (n-1,2?), (n,1) left. Actually we haven't used (n-1,2) maybe used earlier. Let's step back.\n\nWe need a systematic approach. Perhaps we can prove impossibility for k≥4 using a parity or combinatorial argument.\n\nOne approach: Consider the sum of slopes or something? Or consider the fact that any sunny line cannot be parallel to x+y=0, so its slope is not -1. So each sunny line can intersect each of the k rightmost columns at distinct points, but the set of points in each column must be assigned to distinct lines. This is essentially a problem of constructing a system of k lines that intersect each of the k columns at distinct points, covering all points. This is reminiscent of a Latin square: we need to assign each point (i,j) (where i is column index, j is row index) to a line L_t such that each line L_t has at most one point per column. This is like a partial transversal of a rectangular array. For k=3, we found a solution. For k=4, we need to find a transversal of a 4x? shape. Might be impossible due to some combinatorial obstruction.\n\nLet's try to formalize: For columns a = n-k+1,...,n, we have points (a,b) where b = 1,...,n+1-a. So the shape is a right triangle with k rows (since the number of points per column decreases from k to 1). So we have a triangular array of points with k rows (b values) and k columns (a values). The points are (a,b) with a+b ≤ n+1, but for a in [n-k+1,n], b ≤ n+1-a ≤ k. So the set of points is exactly the set of integer points (i,j) with i,j ≥ 1 and i+j ≤ k+1 (if we shift coordinates). Indeed, if we define i = n+1-a, j = b, then i runs from 1 to k, j runs from 1 to i (since a+b ≤ n+1 => (n+1-i)+j ≤ n+1 => j ≤ i). So the points are exactly the triangular set of size k(k+1)/2: points (i,j) with 1 ≤ j ≤ i ≤ k. So the problem reduces to covering the triangular set of points (i,j) with i ≥ j, i ≤ k, using k lines that are not parallel to axes or slope -1 (in original coordinates). In the transformed coordinates, the condition of being sunny translates to lines not being parallel to axes or the line x+y=0 in original coordinates. But after transformation, the condition might be different. However, we can think directly in original coordinates: we have columns a = n-k+1,...,n, and rows b = 1,...,k (but decreasing). The shape is a right triangle.\n\nWe need to cover this triangular set with k lines, each line not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or line x+y=0. So each line must have slope not 0, ∞, -1. So each line can intersect each column at most one point, and each row at most one point (since not horizontal). Also each line cannot be parallel to x+y=0, which means slope not -1, so it cannot intersect each diagonal x+y = constant at more than one point? Actually a line with slope -1 would intersect each diagonal x+y = constant at either zero or infinite points? Actually a line with slope -1 is parallel to the diagonal lines, so it would intersect each diagonal at either zero or infinite points (if coincident). So we cannot have slope -1.\n\nThus each sunny line can intersect each column at most one point, each row at most one point, and each diagonal at at most one point. So each sunny line can be thought of as a \"rook\" in a 3D sense: it picks at most one point per each of three families of parallel lines (vertical, horizontal, diagonal). This is reminiscent of a \"non-attacking\" condition for three directions.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: can we place k non-attacking rooks (with respect to three directions) on a triangular board of size k? For k=3, we found a solution. For k=4, maybe impossible due to combinatorial constraints.\n\nWe can try to prove impossibility for k≥4 using a counting argument: The triangular board has k(k+1)/2 points. Each sunny line can cover at most 3 points? Actually a sunny line can intersect each column at most one point, each row at most one point, each diagonal at at most one point. So a sunny line can cover at most min(k, k, something) points? Actually the maximum number of points a sunny line can cover in this triangular board is k, because there are k columns, and each column can contribute at most one point. So a sunny line can cover at most k points (one per column). But also each row has at most one point per line, but there are k rows, so that also yields at most k points. So the maximum is k. So each line can cover at most k points. So total capacity of k lines is at most k^2 points. The board has k(k+1)/2 points, which is less than k^2 for k≥2. So capacity is not a problem.\n\nBut there may be a more subtle combinatorial obstruction: The triangular board has a property that any set of k lines with slopes not 0,∞,-1 cannot cover all points because of some parity or something.\n\nLet's try to see if we can find a construction for k=4. Perhaps we can find a solution. Let's try to systematically search for a configuration for k=4. Since the problem is small, we can try to find a set of 4 lines that cover the 10 points. Let's try to find a solution manually.\n\nWe need to assign each point to a line. Let's denote the points as (i,j) where i = column index from 1 to 4 (where i=1 corresponds to column n-3, i=2 to n-2, i=3 to n-1, i=4 to n). And j = row index (b) from 1 to (5-i) maybe? Actually for column i (starting from leftmost of the triangle), the number of points is (k+1-i). For k=4, column i has (5-i) points. So:\n\n- Column 1 (i=1): points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\n- Column 2 (i=2): points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\n- Column 3 (i=3): points (3,1),(3,2)\n- Column 4 (i=4): point (4,1)\n\nWe need to find 4 lines (sunny) that cover all these points. Let's denote lines L1,...,L4. Each line can intersect each column at most one point. So each line can be represented by a function mapping column i to a row j_i (or no point). So each line is a selection of at most one point per column, such that the selected points are collinear with slope not 0,∞,-1.\n\nThus we need to partition the set of points into 4 subsets, each subset being a set of points that lie on a line with slope not 0,∞,-1.\n\nWe can try to find a partition.\n\nOne approach: Use lines with slope 1 (45 degrees). As we saw earlier, slope 1 lines correspond to points with constant (j - i) = constant. Let's compute j - i for each point:\n\n- (1,1): 0\n- (1,2): 1\n- (1,3): 2\n- (1,4): 3\n- (2,1): -1\n- (2,2): 0\n- (2,3): 1\n- (3,1): -2\n- (3,2): -1\n- (4,1): -3\n\nSo points with same j-i are collinear with slope 1. Group them:\n\n- j-i = 3: (1,4) only.\n- j-i = 2: (1,3) only.\n- j-i = 1: (1,2), (2,3) -> line L_a.\n- j-i = 0: (1,1), (2,2) -> line L_b.\n- j-i = -1: (2,1), (3,2) -> line L_c.\n- j-i = -2: (3,1) only.\n- j-i = -3: (4,1) only.\n\nThus we have lines: L_a covering (1,2),(2,3); L_b covering (1,1),(2,2); L_c covering (2,1),(3,2). That's 3 lines covering 6 points. Remaining points: (1,3),(1,4),(3,1),(4,1). That's 4 points left, need to be covered by one line (since we have only 4 lines total). But they are not collinear (they are not on a line with slope not 0,∞,-1). So slope 1 lines alone not enough.\n\nWe can try lines with slope -2 (or other slopes). Let's try to find a line that covers (1,4) and (4,1). Slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, which is prohibited. So cannot pair (1,4) with (4,1). How about (1,4) with (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, which is allowed (not -1). So line through (1,4) and (3,1) has slope -3/2. Does it also pass through any other points? Let's check (2,? ) maybe (2,? ) = y = -3/2 (x-1) + 4 = -3/2 (x-1) + 4. For x=2, y = -3/2 (1) + 4 = 4 - 1.5 = 2.5, not integer. So line covers only (1,4) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we have (1,3) and (4,1) left. Can we pair (1,3) with (4,1)? Slope = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3 ≈ -0.666..., which is not -1,0,∞, so allowed. So line through (1,3) and (4,1) has slope -2/3. Does it pass through any other points? For x=2, y = -2/3 (2-1) + 3 = -2/3 + 3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. For x=3, y = -2/3 (3-1) + 3 = -4/3 + 3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666, not integer. So line covers only (1,3) and (4,1). So we have lines:\n\n- L1: (1,2)-(2,3) slope 1\n- L2: (1,1)-(2,2) slope 1\n- L3: (2,1)-(3,2) slope 1\n- L4: (1,4)-(3,1) slope -3/2\n- L5: (1,3)-(4,1) slope -2/3\n\nThat's 5 lines, but we need only 4 lines. So we need to combine some of these lines.\n\nMaybe we can combine (1,4)-(3,1) with (2,2) or something to get a line covering three points. Let's see if there is a line that passes through (1,4), (2,2), (3,1). Check slopes: (2,2)-(1,4) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2/1 = -2. (3,1)-(2,2) slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1/1 = -1, which is prohibited. So (2,2)-(3,1) slope -1, not allowed. So cannot have all three.\n\nWhat about line through (1,4), (2,3), (3,2), (4,1)? That's slope -1 (since points (i,j) with i+j=5). Indeed (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1) lie on line x+y=5, which has slope -1, prohibited. So cannot use that line.\n\nThus we need to find a different partition.\n\nMaybe we can use lines with slope -2 (or other slopes) to cover more points.\n\nLet's try to find a line that covers (1,4) and (2,2). Slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. Does it also pass through (3,? ) maybe (3,0) which is not in set. So line L_a: (1,4)-(2,2). That covers those two points.\n\nNow we have (1,3),(1,2),(1,1),(1,? ) Actually we have points left: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3) (since (1,4) used), (2,1),(2,3) (since (2,2) used), (3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 8 points left.\n\nWe need to cover them with 3 lines. Let's try to find lines that cover many points.\n\nConsider line through (1,1) and (2,3): slope = (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, allowed. Does it also pass through (3,5)? Not in set. So L_b: (1,1)-(2,3). That covers those two.\n\nNow left: (1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 6 points left, need to cover with 2 lines. That's likely impossible because each line can cover at most 4 points (since there are 4 columns). But we have 6 points left, maybe each line can cover 3 points. Let's try to find a line covering (1,2),(3,1),(4,1)? (3,1) and (4,1) are horizontal line y=1, prohibited. So cannot.\n\nTry line covering (1,2),(2,1),(3,? ) maybe (3,0) not in set. So L_c: (1,2)-(2,1) slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, prohibited. So cannot.\n\nTry line covering (1,3),(2,1),(3,? ) maybe (3,-1) not in set. Slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So line L_c: (1,3)-(2,1) slope -2. Does it also pass through (3,-1)? Not in set. So covers those two.\n\nNow left: (1,2),(3,2),(4,1). That's 3 points left, need to be covered by one line. Check if they are collinear: slope between (1,2) and (3,2) is (2-2)/(3-1) = 0, prohibited (horizontal). So cannot. So this partition fails.\n\nLet's try a different approach: maybe we can use a line that covers three points: (1,2),(2,3),(3,4?) but (3,4) not in set. So not.\n\nMaybe we can use a line with slope -1/2: (1,4) and (3,3)? (3,3) not in set. So not.\n\nWe need to systematically search for a solution for k=4. Since the problem is small, we could try to brute force search for lines. But we can try to reason about impossibility.\n\nOne approach: Use a combinatorial argument based on the fact that each sunny line can intersect each of the three families of parallel lines (vertical, horizontal, diagonal) at most once. In the triangular region, there are k vertical lines (columns), k horizontal lines (rows), and k diagonals (lines x+y = constant). The points are exactly the intersection of these families: each point lies at the intersection of a unique column, row, and diagonal. So we have a 3D matching problem: we need to assign each point to a line such that each line picks at most one point from each column, each row, each diagonal. This is like a 3-dimensional rook placement problem: we need to place k rooks (lines) such that each rook occupies a set of points that are pairwise non-attacking in three directions. Actually each line can be thought of as a set of points that are pairwise non-attacking in the sense that they lie on the same line, but the line itself is not parallel to any of the three families, so it intersects each family exactly once. So each line can be thought of as a \"transversal\" of the three families: it picks one column, one row, one diagonal for each point it contains. Actually each point is defined by a triple (col, row, diag). A line that is not parallel to any of the families will intersect each column at exactly one point (if it intersects that column at all), each row at exactly one point, each diagonal at exactly one point. So a line can be thought of as a set of points where each point has a distinct column, distinct row, distinct diagonal. So each line corresponds to a permutation of the columns to rows to diagonals? Actually each line can be represented by a linear equation y = mx + b with m not 0,∞,-1. For each column x = a, the line gives a unique y = ma + b. So the point (a, ma+b) is the intersection of column a and the line. If that point is integer and within the triangular region, then it's a point covered. So each line picks at most one point per column, and the row index is determined by the column index via the line equation. So each line corresponds to a function mapping column index a to row index b = ma + b0 (where b0 is intercept). So the set of points covered by a line is the set of integer points (a,b) satisfying b = ma + b0, with a integer between 1 and k, and b integer between 1 and k, and also b ≤ a (since triangular region). So each line corresponds to a linear function with slope m not 0,∞,-1, and intercept b0 such that the points (a,ma+b0) are integer and satisfy b ≤ a.\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering all integer points (a,b) with 1 ≤ b ≤ a ≤ k using k lines of the form b = m_i a + b_i, with m_i not 0,∞,-1, and each line can only cover points where b is integer. So we need to find k such lines that cover all points.\n\nFor k=3, we found lines: slope 1 (b = a + c?), slope -1/2, slope -2. Let's see: For k=3, points are (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). The lines we used: L1: (1,1)-(2,2) slope 1 => b = a (since passes through (1,1) and (2,2)). L2: (1,2)-(3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 => b = -1/2 a + 2.5? Actually b = -0.5 a + 2.5. For a=1, b=2; a=3, b=1. So line passes through (1,2) and (3,1). L3: (1,3)-(2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2 => b = -2 a + 5. For a=1, b=3; a=2, b=1. So indeed these three lines cover all points.\n\nNow for k=4, we need 4 lines covering points (a,b) with 1 ≤ b ≤ a ≤ 4. Let's list points:\n\na=1: b=1,2,3,4\na=2: b=1,2,3\na=3: b=1,2\na=4: b=1\n\nTotal 10 points.\n\nWe need 4 lines of the form b = m a + c, with m not 0,∞,-1, covering all points.\n\nWe can try to find such lines. Let's denote lines L_i: b = m_i a + c_i.\n\nWe need to assign each point (a,b) to some line such that b = m_i a + c_i for that line.\n\nThus we need to find 4 linear functions (with integer outputs for integer a) that cover all points.\n\nSince b must be integer, m_i a + c_i must be integer for each a where the line covers a point. So m_i must be rational (since a integer). So we can consider m_i = p_i/q_i in lowest terms, and c_i rational as well.\n\nWe need to ensure that for each a, the line yields integer b within [1,a].\n\nWe can try to find a systematic way.\n\nOne approach: Use lines with slopes 1, -1/2, -2, and maybe something else. For k=4, we might try to use slopes 1, -1/3, -3, maybe something else.\n\nLet's try to see if we can extend the pattern: For k=3, we used slopes 1, -1/2, -2. For k=4, maybe we can use slopes 1, -1/3, -3, and something else? Let's test.\n\nLine with slope 1: b = a + c. For integer points, we need c integer offset. For points (a,b) with b = a + c, we need b ≤ a, so c ≤ 0. Also b ≥ 1, so a + c ≥ 1 => c ≥ 1 - a. For a=1, c ≥ 0. So c can be 0 or negative. For c=0, line is b = a, which passes through points (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). But (4,4) is not in our set because b ≤ a, but a=4, b=4 is allowed? Actually for a=4, b can be up to 4? Wait, the triangular region is b ≤ a, but also b ≤ k? Actually we have b ≤ a, and a ≤ k. So (4,4) is allowed? Let's check original points: For column a=4 (i.e., original column n), we have only point (4,1). Wait, we need to be careful: In the triangular region after transformation, the points are (i,j) with 1 ≤ j ≤ i ≤ k. So (4,4) is allowed: i=4, j=4. But does that correspond to a point (a,b) in original coordinates? Let's map back: original coordinates: a = n - i + 1? Actually we defined i = n+1 - a, j = b. So a = n+1 - i. For i=4, a = n+1-4 = n-3. So column a = n-3 corresponds to i=4. So (i,j) = (4,4) corresponds to original point (a,b) = (n-3,4). Indeed, column n-3 has points (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-3,4). So (n-3,4) is indeed a point. So (4,4) is in the set. So line b = a (i.e., j = i) passes through points (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). That covers (n-3,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,3),(n,4)? Wait, mapping back: (i,j) = (1,1) => a = n+1-1 = n, b = 1 => point (n,1). (2,2) => a = n+1-2 = n-1, b = 2 => (n-1,2). (3,3) => a = n+1-3 = n-2, b = 3 => (n-2,3). (4,4) => a = n+1-4 = n-3, b = 4 => (n-3,4). So line b = a (i.e., j = i) corresponds to points (n,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,3),(n-3,4). That's a diagonal line with slope -1 in original coordinates (since x+y = n+1). Indeed, those points lie on line x+y = n+1, which is slope -1, which is prohibited (non-sunny). So we cannot use line b = a because it's slope -1 in original coordinates. So slope 1 in transformed coordinates corresponds to slope -1 in original coordinates, which is not allowed. Wait, we need to be careful: The transformation we used (i = n+1-a, j = b) changes the orientation. In original coordinates, a is x coordinate, b is y coordinate. The line x+y = constant has slope -1. In transformed coordinates, the line j = i corresponds to original line x+y = constant (since j = i => b = n+1-a => a+b = n+1). So indeed, slope 1 in transformed coordinates corresponds to slope -1 in original coordinates, which is prohibited. So we cannot use lines with slope 1 in transformed coordinates. However, in the solution for k=3, we used lines with slope 1 in original coordinates? Let's check: For k=3, we used line through (1,1)-(2,2) which has slope 1 in original coordinates. That line is not prohibited (since slope 1 is allowed). In transformed coordinates, that line would have slope something else. Let's compute transformation: i = n+1-a, j = b. For points (a,b) = (1,1) => i = n, j = 1. For (2,2) => i = n-1, j = 2. So line connecting (i,j) = (n,1) and (n-1,2) has slope (2-1)/(n-1 - n) = 1/(-1) = -1. So in transformed coordinates, that line has slope -1, which is prohibited in transformed coordinates? But we didn't impose any restriction in transformed coordinates; the restriction is only in original coordinates. So we cannot directly map the problem to transformed coordinates and impose the same restrictions. So we need to be careful.\n\nThus, the transformation approach may not be helpful.\n\nInstead, we can think directly in original coordinates: we have columns a = n-k+1,...,n. For each column a, we have points (a,b) for b = 1,...,n+1-a. So the shape is a right triangle with right angle at (n,1). The columns are decreasing in height from left to right.\n\nWe need to cover these points with k lines that are not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or line x+y=0. So each line can intersect each column at most one point, each row at most one point, and each diagonal (x+y = constant) at most one point. So each line can be thought of as a \"non-attacking\" line in three directions.\n\nThus, the problem reduces to covering the triangular board with k non-attacking rooks (lines) where each rook can occupy multiple squares (points) as long as they are on the same line. Actually each line can occupy multiple points, but those points must be in distinct columns, rows, and diagonals. So each line corresponds to a set of points that form a \"partial transversal\" of the three families.\n\nThus, the problem is to partition the triangular board into k such transversals.\n\nFor k=3, we found a partition. For k=4, we need to partition the 10 points into 4 transversals. Is that possible? Let's try to find a partition manually.\n\nWe need to assign each point to one of 4 lines. Let's denote the lines as L1, L2, L3, L4. Each line can have at most one point per column, per row, per diagonal.\n\nWe can think of each line as a permutation of columns to rows (since each line picks at most one point per column, and each point has a row index). So each line corresponds to a partial injection from columns to rows. Since each line cannot have two points in the same row, the mapping must be injective. So each line corresponds to a partial matching between columns and rows. Also, each line cannot have two points on the same diagonal (x+y constant). So the mapping must also avoid same sum a+b.\n\nThus, each line corresponds to a set of points (a,b) such that a's are distinct, b's are distinct, and a+b's are distinct. So each line is a set of points that form a \"partial permutation\" with distinct sums.\n\nThus, the problem reduces to partitioning the set of points (a,b) with a+b ≤ n+1 into k subsets, each subset being a set of points with distinct a, distinct b, distinct a+b.\n\nThis is reminiscent of a known combinatorial design: the set of points in a triangular board can be partitioned into k \"non-attacking\" rooks (like a Latin square of order k). Indeed, the set of points (a,b) with a+b ≤ k+1 can be partitioned into k \"non-attacking\" rooks (i.e., each subset is a set of points with distinct a,b,a+b). This is known as a \"triangular Latin square\" or \"Steiner system\"? Actually it's known that the triangular board of size k can be partitioned into k \"non-attacking\" rooks (i.e., a set of k permutations of size k). For k=3, we have 3 lines each covering 2 points (except one covering 2?). Actually we had lines covering 2 points each: L1: (1,1)-(2,2) (distinct a,b,a+b: a+b = 2,4? Actually (1,1) sum=2, (2,2) sum=4, distinct). L2: (1,2)-(3,1) sum=3,4? Actually (1,2) sum=3, (3,1) sum=4, distinct. L3: (1,3)-(2,1) sum=4,3? Actually (1,3) sum=4, (2,1) sum=3, distinct. So each line has distinct sums.\n\nFor k=4, we need to partition 10 points into 4 subsets, each subset with distinct a,b,a+b. The maximum size of a subset is at most 4 (since there are 4 columns). So we need to partition 10 points into 4 subsets, with sizes maybe 3,3,2,2 (since 3+3+2+2 = 10). Or 4,3,2,1, etc. But each subset must have distinct a,b,a+b.\n\nWe can try to find such a partition.\n\nLet's list all points with their (a,b) and sum s = a+b:\n\n- (1,1) s=2\n- (1,2) s=3\n- (1,3) s=4\n- (1,4) s=5\n- (2,1) s=3\n- (2,2) s=4\n- (2,3) s=5\n- (3,1) s=4\n- (3,2) s=5\n- (4,1) s=5\n\nWe need to partition these into 4 subsets, each subset with distinct a values (i.e., at most one point per column), distinct b values (at most one per row), distinct s values (at most one per diagonal). So each subset is a partial matching of columns to rows with distinct sums.\n\nWe can think of constructing a 4x4 matrix with rows b=1..4, columns a=1..4, but only entries where b ≤ a are allowed (triangular). We need to fill the matrix with numbers 1..4 (line indices) such that each number appears at most once in each row, column, and diagonal (i.e., sum). This is like a partial Latin square with diagonal constraints.\n\nWe need to assign each of the 10 allowed cells a label from {1,2,3,4} such that each label appears at most once per row, column, diagonal. This is a known problem: can we fill a triangular board with 4 symbols such that each symbol appears at most once per row, column, diagonal? This is like a \"Sudoku\" variant.\n\nWe can try to find such a labeling.\n\nLet's denote cells:\n\n- (1,1) (col1,row1, sum2)\n- (1,2) (col1,row2,sum3)\n- (1,3) (col1,row3,sum4)\n- (1,4) (col1,row4,sum5)\n- (2,1) (col2,row1,sum3)\n- (2,2) (col2,row2,sum4)\n- (2,3) (col2,row3,sum5)\n- (3,1) (col3,row1,sum4)\n- (3,2) (col3,row2,sum5)\n- (4,1) (col4,row1,sum5)\n\nWe need to assign labels 1-4.\n\nConstraints: For each label L, at most one cell per column (col), at most one per row, at most one per sum.\n\nThus, each label corresponds to a set of cells that form a partial transversal of the three partitions.\n\nWe need to assign each cell a label such that each label appears at most once per column, row, sum.\n\nWe can try to find a labeling manually.\n\nLet's try to assign label 1 to (1,1). Then label 1 cannot appear in column 1 again, row 1 again, sum 2 again. So we cannot assign label 1 to any other cell in column 1 (i.e., (1,2),(1,3),(1,4)), row 1 (i.e., (2,1),(3,1),(4,1)), or sum 2 (only (1,1) has sum 2). So label 1 is used only at (1,1) for now.\n\nNow assign label 2 to (1,2). Then label 2 cannot appear in column 1, row 2, sum 3. So cannot assign label 2 to (1,3),(1,4) (col1), (2,2) (row2), (3,2) (row2), (2,1) (sum3), (3,? ) Actually sum 3 cells are (1,2) and (2,1). So label 2 cannot be at (2,1). So far okay.\n\nAssign label 3 to (1,3). Then label 3 cannot appear in column 1, row 3, sum 4. Sum 4 cells are (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). So label 3 cannot be at (2,2) or (3,1). Row 3 cells are (1,3),(2,3). So label 3 cannot be at (2,3). Column 1 cells are (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). So label 3 cannot be at (1,4). So far okay.\n\nAssign label 4 to (1,4). Then label 4 cannot appear in column 1, row 4, sum 5. Sum 5 cells are (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). So label 4 cannot be at (2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Row 4 cells: only (1,4). So okay.\n\nNow we have assigned all cells in column 1. So column 1 is fully assigned with labels 1,2,3,4. Now we need to assign labels to remaining cells: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). But we must respect constraints: each label can appear at most once per column, row, sum. So far, each label appears once in column 1. So they cannot appear again in column 1 (which is fine). They can appear in other columns, but each label can appear at most once per column, so they can appear in column 2,3,4 as long as they haven't been used there yet. Also each label can appear at most once per row: we have used label 1 in row 1 (at (1,1)), label 2 in row 2 (at (1,2)), label 3 in row 3 (at (1,3)), label 4 in row 4 (at (1,4)). So each row already has a label assigned. So we cannot assign any label to another cell in the same row. So we cannot assign label 1 to any other cell in row 1 (i.e., (2,1),(3,1),(4,1)). So label 1 cannot be used in any of those cells. Similarly, label 2 cannot be used in row 2 cells (i.e., (2,2),(3,2)). Label 3 cannot be used in row 3 cells (i.e., (2,3)). Label 4 cannot be used in row 4 cells (none left). So we have constraints.\n\nAlso each label cannot be used in same sum: label 1 used sum 2, label 2 sum 3, label 3 sum 4, label 4 sum 5. So they cannot be used in any other cell with same sum.\n\nNow let's list remaining cells with their row, column, sum:\n\n- (2,1): col2, row1, sum3\n- (2,2): col2, row2, sum4\n- (2,3): col2, row3, sum5\n- (3,1): col3, row1, sum4\n- (3,2): col3, row2, sum5\n- (4,1): col4, row1, sum5\n\nWe need to assign labels 1-4 to these cells, respecting constraints.\n\nConstraints summary:\n\n- Label 1 cannot be used in row1 (since used at (1,1)), cannot be used in sum2 (already used), cannot be used in column1 (already used). So label 1 can be used in any remaining cell that is not in row1, sum2, column1. Row1 cells among remaining: (2,1),(3,1),(4,1) are all row1, so label 1 cannot be used in any of those. So label 1 cannot be used anywhere else, because all remaining cells are in row1, row2, row3. Actually label 1 cannot be used in row1, but can be used in row2 or row3. Let's see: remaining cells in row2: (2,2),(3,2). Row3: (2,3). So label 1 could be placed in (2,2) or (3,2) or (2,3) if other constraints allow. Also sum constraints: label 1 used sum2, so cannot be used in any other cell with sum2. None of the remaining cells have sum2 (they have sums 3,4,5). So okay. Column constraints: label 1 used column1, so cannot be used in column1 (none left). So label 1 can be placed in any of (2,2),(3,2),(2,3) as long as row constraints are satisfied (i.e., not row1). So label 1 can be placed in (2,2) (row2), (3,2) (row2), or (2,3) (row3). But we also need to ensure that each column gets at most one label 1. So we can place label 1 in at most one of column2, column3, column4. So we need to decide.\n\n- Label 2 cannot be used in row2 (since used at (1,2)), cannot be used in sum3 (since used at (1,2)), cannot be used in column1. So label 2 can be used in cells not in row2, sum3. Remaining cells: (2,1) row1 sum3 (sum3 is prohibited for label 2), so cannot use label 2 at (2,1). (2,2) row2 sum4 (row2 prohibited), cannot. (2,3) row3 sum5 (allowed: row3 not row2, sum5 not sum3). So label 2 could be placed at (2,3). (3,1) row1 sum4 (allowed: row1 not row2, sum4 not sum3). So label 2 could be placed at (3,1). (3,2) row2 sum5 (row2 prohibited). (4,1) row1 sum5 (allowed). So label 2 could be placed at (3,1) or (4,1) or (2,3). But we need to ensure column constraints: label 2 can appear at most once per column. So we need to pick one column for label 2.\n\n- Label 3 cannot be used in row3 (since used at (1,3)), cannot be used in sum4 (since used at (1,3)), cannot be used in column1. So label 3 can be used in cells not in row3, sum4. Remaining cells: (2,1) row1 sum3 (allowed: row1 not row3, sum3 not sum4). So label 3 could be at (2,1). (2,2) row2 sum4 (sum4 prohibited). So cannot. (2,3) row3 sum5 (row3 prohibited). So cannot. (3,1) row1 sum4 (sum4 prohibited). So cannot. (3,2) row2 sum5 (allowed). So label 3 could be at (3,2). (4,1) row1 sum5 (allowed). So label 3 could be at (4,1). So possible placements for label 3: (2,1), (3,2), (4,1). Need column constraint.\n\n- Label 4 cannot be used in row4 (since used at (1,4)), cannot be used in sum5 (since used at (1,4)), cannot be used in column1. So label 4 can be used in cells not in row4, sum5. Remaining cells: (2,1) row1 sum3 (allowed). (2,2) row2 sum4 (allowed). (2,3) row3 sum5 (sum5 prohibited). So cannot. (3,1) row1 sum4 (allowed). (3,2) row2 sum5 (sum5 prohibited). So cannot. (4,1) row1 sum5 (sum5 prohibited). So cannot. So label 4 can be placed at (2,1), (2,2), (3,1). Need column constraint.\n\nNow we need to assign each remaining cell a label such that each label appears exactly once in each column? Actually each column can have at most one of each label, but we need to assign each cell a label, so each column will have multiple labels (since there are multiple cells in each column). 